<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>$(LibraryTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
    <!--
      The mock gateway *compresses*, to serve the zstd-framed JSONL bodies several historical
      endpoints return; the library only ever decompresses. Same reasoning as the Live test
      project's copy of this reference: CLAUDE.md's rule that every zstd call goes through
      Internal/ZstdDecompressor.cs is about the shipping codec, and adding an unused compression
      seam to the library so a test double could call it would work against that rule rather than
      with it.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="ZstdSharp.Port" />
    <!--
      The one timeout this harness owns, per PORTING.md §2: anywhere a TimeSpan would have been
      reached for, the answer in this repo is NodaTime's Duration — and BannedSymbols.txt makes
      that a build error rather than a habit, in test projects too.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Project-scoped ban, on top of the root BannedSymbols.txt: see that file for the D7 rationale.
    BannedApiAnalyzers reads every AdditionalFiles entry named BannedSymbols.txt, so this adds to
    the root file's bans rather than replacing them.

    $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory), not a bare relative Include: csc receives whatever path the
    Include produces verbatim as its /additionalfile: argument rather than resolving it against
    the project directory first, so a bare "BannedSymbols.txt" is silently unreadable from
    wherever the compiler's working directory happens to be — no error, no diagnostic, just a
    ban that never fires. Confirmed by hand while writing this file: with a bare Include, a
    MetadataEncoder reference built clean with no warning at all. The root file's own
    AdditionalFiles entry in Directory.Build.props already uses this same prefix.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AdditionalFiles Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)BannedSymbols.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Kestrel — and this is the whole reason the historical test double is a real server rather than
    an HttpMessageHandler stub. A fake handler never opens a socket, so it cannot exercise chunked
    transfer and back-pressure, a `Range: bytes=N-` answered `206 Partial Content`, a connection
    dropped mid-body, or HttpClient itself — and HttpClient is the component under test in half of
    M3. See ROADMAP.md §5 and PORTING.md §2.

    A FrameworkReference, not a PackageReference, so Directory.Packages.props needs no new entry:
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.App is a shared framework that ships with the SDK, not a package that is
    restored. Rejected alternatives were WireMock.Net (a third-party dependency wrapping this same
    server, plus a DSL we would use for little more than "match this path, return this body") and
    HttpListener (no new dependency, but the legacy stack with the weakest streaming and Range
    story).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    This project now reaches DatabentoDotNet.Dbn — transitively, through the ProjectReference
    below, because #35 gave DatabentoDotNet.Historical its own reference to the codec (the zstd
    seam, and the composition test Task 3 owes needs Symbols). Before #35 this comment said the
    rule was "no path to DatabentoDotNet.Dbn"; that claim is false the moment the reference below
    exists, so the build no longer enforces it by omission and restating it here would be lying
    about what protects the independent-oracle rule now.

    D7 (#35) is the restatement: the rule never actually meant "this project cannot see the
    codec" — it meant this harness must not manufacture the bytes it serves with the codec those
    bytes exist to check. SyntheticDbnFragment hand-builds DBN by hand for exactly that reason,
    and that is what must keep being true, not the absence of a reference. What the independent
    reads guard against is DatabentoDotNet.Dbn.MetadataEncoder specifically: a double that built
    its fixtures with the same codec it exists to check would let a misreading of DBN's wire
    format sit in both and have them agree with each other, the same reasoning the mock gateway
    itself must never use the client it exists to test. The Live harness makes the same argument
    for the client it still does not reference at all.

    So the rule is now enforced by BannedSymbols.txt in this directory, banning
    T:DatabentoDotNet.Dbn.MetadataEncoder specifically — see that file. Symbols is deliberately
    *not* banned: nothing about building a Symbols value touches the bytes this harness serves,
    and the composition test this issue owes needs it. #34's other reason for no reference still
    holds independently of any of this: this harness is written from Databento's HTTP
    documentation rather than from our client, which is why the User-Agent assertion matches the
    literal prefix `DatabentoDotNet/` rather than calling the library's UserAgent type.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/DatabentoDotNet.Historical/DatabentoDotNet.Historical.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
